    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "-rom Lorrin. Thurstan\n• АРП\nHonolulu, Hawat. PM\nCALL\n3 DE\n4. PM\nCRANES\nSPECIAL A DELIVERY\n20..\n1235\nd.S. Unstage\n→ APR 3\nCALI\nOPEN GRAVIS (5)\n193\nVEROS AGE\n1930\nDUDS VEST\n14_PM\n8\nPan American Airways Clipper Ship\nHonolulu, Hawaii, April 22, 1935\nPhim Rusek\n10. J. rachae\nanklanaday\nCommander\n2ad in Command\nNavigator\nEngineer\nRadioman\nJunior Officer\nFRANC,\n2 APR23 2\n\"4PM\nCALIE\n1925.\n(50.\nAPR 22\n2\nU\n1.DOSTAGE\nAFR 22\nONO\nAPR 22\n2 PM\nHAWAY\nAFR 22\nTo Pan American Airways\nHonolulu, Hawaii\nFRANKLIN D. ROOSEVELT\nPRESIDENT OF THE LINITED STATES\nWHITE HOUSE\nWASHINGTON D.C.\nFIRST FLICHT\nPAN AMERICAN CLIPPER\n193",
      "ROOSEVELT (FDR) Stamp collection. Large size\nflight cover of the first flight by Pan-Ameri-\ncan Clipper from Hawaii to California on 4/23/\n1935. The stamps were all valid stamps \u0026 comple-\nte as of April 23rd 1935. Also the signatures of\nthe entire flight crew .The cover was bought at\nthe auction of the postage stamp\ncollection of\nFDR sold by H.R. Harmer, N.Y 7/16/1946. The en-\nclosed letter refers to the signatures of the\nflight's crew \u0026 the catalog announcing the sale\nof the cover.\n10/75\nHE F. D. ROOSEVELT COLLECTION\n68\nJUL IG 1946\nROOSEVELT STAMPS\nYIELD $17,200 MORE\nBoxes\nand\ncases\ncontaining\nthousands of stamps and covers\nthat the late Franklin D. Roosevelt\nnever found time to classify for his\nalbums were included among near-\nly 400 lots that brought $17,200 at\npublic auction yesterday\nthrough\nH. R. Harmer, Inc., 32 East Fifty-\nseventh Street. This was the third\nand final section to go under the\nhammer.\nBernard\nHarmer\nhad\nhoped it would bring from\n$5,000\nto $10,000, but collectors and deal-\ners paid many times normal cata-\nlogue values for some lots.\nAn outstanding cover t\ncover was one\nposted by\nEustatius,\nMr.\nat\nSt.\nCuraçao, in 1936 while\naboard athe cruiser Houston: ad-\ndressed to himself and bearing his\nautograph, it brought $310. A 1939\nNew\nYork\nWorld's\nFair\ncove\nsigned by the President and others\nwent\nfor $150.\nA 1935\nairmail 1\ncover from William C. Bullitt, then\nAmbassador to Bussia, brought!\n$110.\nA cardboard box containing\n2,800\ncovers\naddressed\nthe\nPresident\nfrom\nall parts\nof the\nworld realized $1,150.\nThe\nday's\ntotal\nof\nbrought\nto $228,000\nthe\n$17,200\nincome\nthat the Roosevelt estate realized,\nminus\ntaxes\nand\ncommissions,\nfrom the sale of the collection the\nlâte President accumulated from\nboyhood.\nFROM THE FRANKLIN D. ROOSEVELT COLLECTION\nAUTHENTICATED BY H, R. HARMER INC., N. Y.\nEROM THE ERANKLIN D. ROOSEVELT COLLECTION\nANTHENTIGATED BY HI HARMER INC, N. Y."
    ],
